# Line endings for the generated trees.
#
# Four of this repository's checks are `git diff --exit-code` over regenerated
# output. On a Windows checkout git converts those files to CRLF, the generator
# then writes LF, and the guard reports drift in files whose content is
# identical — which has now cost two separate diagnoses: openapi-check failed on
# a description string that differed by nothing but an escaped carriage return,
# and ceremony-ui-check failed on a trailing newline.
#
# `eol=lf` pins them. The generators all emit LF, so the working copy now
# matches what they produce and a guard failing means something really changed.
# Scoped to generated and guarded paths rather than applied repository-wide,
# because renormalising everything would produce one enormous diff that hides
# whatever is in it.

# Protobuf output, guarded by proto-check.
x/**/*.pb.go         text eol=lf
x/**/*.pb.gw.go      text eol=lf

# Reference documentation, guarded by docs-check.
docs/reference/**    text eol=lf

# The OpenAPI specification, guarded by openapi-check.
docs/static/openapi.json  text eol=lf

# TypeScript bindings, guarded by proto-ts-check.
clients/sdk/src/generated/**  text eol=lf

# The hosted ceremony bundle, guarded by ceremony-ui-check. This one matters
# beyond tidiness: the host hashes these bytes and the page compares its own
# script against that digest, so a line-ending conversion would make an honest
# page look like a substituted one.
tools/ceremony/hosted/**  text eol=lf

# Rendered documentation for the website. Not guarded, but generated, so it
# drifts for the same reason.
clients/site/docs/**  text eol=lf

# Shell scripts run on Linux hosts. A CRLF here is not a cosmetic problem —
# `#!/bin/bash\r` is not an interpreter anything can find, and the error names
# the script rather than the line ending.
*.sh                 text eol=lf

# The foundation console. Same reasoning as *.sh, one step removed: this page's
# output *is* shell commands, composed from template literals in its own source,
# and a custodian copies them into a terminal. A CRLF checkout puts a carriage
# return inside every composed command, and pasting that gives
# `$'\r': command not found` — an error that names nothing useful and appears
# only for the person whose machine served the page.
clients/foundation/**  text eol=lf

# The other three operator consoles, for exactly the same reason. Every one of
# them composes shell commands for somebody to paste — the land registry prints
# `blockchaind tx land …` for an office, the governance console prints the
# `submit-proposal` and the follow-up queries, and the validator console prints
# a vote — and every one of them is deployed by copying the working tree to the
# host rather than by exporting from git, so the bytes a Windows checkout holds
# are the bytes the browser gets. These were unpinned while the foundation was
# pinned, which is not a distinction any of them earns.
clients/governance/**  text eol=lf
clients/land/**        text eol=lf
clients/validator/**   text eol=lf

# The hosted ceremony's HTML shell. Vite copies this file into
# tools/ceremony/hosted/ almost verbatim, and that output is the bundle whose
# bytes the host hashes so a custodian's page can verify itself against a digest
# published out of band. A CRLF here therefore reaches the digest: two operators
# on different platforms compute different hashes for an identical, honest page,
# and a mismatch is indistinguishable from substitution — which is the one thing
# that check exists to detect. Pinned at the source, because the generated copy
# inherits whatever this file has.
clients/ceremony/index.html  text eol=lf
